# Theorem 1.2 for the infinite family `C_{36,k}`

Exact polynomial certificate of §5 / Appendix A, as a genuine Lean proof.

* Contact polynomials `Q20 Q21 Q22 : ℚ[X]` (`= Q_{36,20}, Q_{36,21}, Q_{36,22}`
  of Proposition 3.1) are embedded from `certs/q36.json`.
* Log-concavity difference `G = Q21^2 - Q20*Q22` satisfies the shift certificate
  `G(110+x) = -(1/𝒟) ∑_{j=0}^{42} b_j x^j` with all `b_j > 0` (equation (30)).
* Therefore `G(u) < 0` for every integer `u ≥ 110`, and `k ≥ 10` implies
  `k(k+1) ≥ 110`, which is exactly
  `n_21(C_{36,k})^2 < n_20(C_{36,k}) n_22(C_{36,k})`.

Also proved: the leading-coefficient test (26) and the necessary condition (23)
both fail at `(r,h) = (36,21)`.

No `sorry`, no `native_decide`. Certificate arithmetic is kernel `decide` /
`norm_num` on explicit `ℤ` / `ℚ` literals; algebraic steps use Mathlib
`Polynomial` / `Finset` lemmas.

## Integration points

* Intended companions: `Paper20.Polynomial` (Prop. 3.1 identification
  `n_h(C_{r,k}) = Q_{r,h}(k(k+1))`) and `Paper20.Hilbert.bpsCoeff`.
  This module is self-contained and states Theorem 1.2 both as a pure statement
  about the certified `Q` polynomials and, via `bpsCoeffModel`, against an
  abstract BPS-coefficient interface.
* Strict positivity of every BPS coefficient is Lemma 2.2 (braid-walk bound
  `1 ≤ n_h ≤ binom(2d,2h)`).  It is recorded as a separate interface
  consequence and the braid-walk theory is not rebuilt here.
